# ── SPA fallback — REMOVED (fixed in audit) ──────────────────────────────────
# This used to be:
#   /* /index.html 200
# which meant EVERY path that matched no file — /foo, /blog/typo, /js/missing.js,
# /admin/secrets, /wp-login.php — was answered with the main app's index.html and
# a 200 OK status. Three problems with that:
#   1. The project ships a proper, well-designed 404.html (Arabic RTL, links back
#      to / and /#services). It was 100% unreachable, because nothing ever 404'd.
#   2. Soft-404s: Google treats a 200 response for a non-existent URL as a real
#      page, so any URL in the infinite space under /* can be indexed, and the
#      site looks like it has infinite duplicate content.
#   3. Typos in /blog/<slug> silently showed the homepage instead of an error.
#
# Removing it is SAFE for this app: routing is hash-based, not path-based — every
# in-app deep link is `/#orders`, `/#workspace-<orderId>` etc. (see handleHash()
# in index.html), so the server only ever receives `/` for the app itself. All the
# real paths (/blog/*, /about, /contact, /terms, /privacy) are handled by the
# explicit rules above, and static assets are served directly by Pages.
# Cloudflare now serves 404.html with a genuine 404 status.
#
# To restore the old behaviour instead, uncomment this line:
# /* /index.html 200
